    "problem": "Before the framework, tax-and-spending policy ran without a binding rule: day-to-day deficits could be covered by borrowing year after year, and in past rounds of budget-tightening (consolidations) long-term building projects (capital projects) were often the first thing cut because they were politically easiest to delay. By 1997-98 government debt (public sector net debt) stood at about 40% of GDP, while net public sector investment — government spending on long-term assets like roads and buildings — had collapsed to roughly 0.4% of GDP in 1999, a record low, leaving roads, schools and hospitals under-maintained. The absence of a credible, transparent commitment also left financial markets uncertain whether everyday spending would be paid for from taxes or simply added to the debt.",
