    "problem": "For 205 years after British settlement in 1788, Australian law rested on terra nullius — the idea that the continent had belonged to no one — so it recognised no pre-existing Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander ownership of land. The High Court rejected that idea in 1992, but there was still no national way to act on it. Before 1993, large-scale return of land had happened in only one place: the Northern Territory, where a 1976 federal law had handed back about 44% of the Territory to Aboriginal owners. That law applied nowhere else, and across most of the country Indigenous people had little or no legal means to reclaim their traditional country.",
