    "problem": "Police departments have begun running surveillance-camera footage and photo databases through commercial facial-recognition software to put names to faces. The software is far from perfect, and its mistakes are not spread evenly. In a 2018 test, the American Civil Liberties Union compared the official photo of every U.S. member of Congress against a database of 25,000 arrest mugshots using Amazon's \"Rekognition\" system; the software falsely flagged 28 sitting lawmakers as matches to people who had been arrested. Nearly 40% of those false matches were people of color, even though they make up only about 20% of Congress. A single wrong match can put an innocent person under suspicion, a search, or an arrest.",
